;;; class-ErrorObject.slatl — Motoi Scheme Reference · error-cards
;;; Census key: class:ErrorObject   ·   family: language   ·   counted-real

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "error-card — class:ErrorObject (custom (error ...))")

(error-card :key "class:ErrorObject"
  :name "Error object (raised by (error ...))"
  :family "language"
  :census-status "counted-real"
  :library "error-cards"
  :training-eligible #t
  :confidentiality "public"
  :provenance "scheme/reference/error-cards/class-ErrorObject.slatl"
  :status "real"

  :scheme-classification
"R7RS §6.11: `(error message irritant ...)` raises an ERROR OBJECT — a first-class
value carrying a message string and a list of irritants. It is the STANDARD way a
program signals its own failure. `error-object?`, `error-object-message`, and
`error-object-irritants` inspect it; `guard` catches it. Motoi tags these
internally with the class name `ErrorObject`, and because no language-family
regex matches an arbitrary user message, the census buckets them under
`class:ErrorObject`."

  :trigger-code "(error \"boom\" 1 2)"
  :observed-output "error: boom 1 2"
  :normalizes-to "class:ErrorObject"

  :meaning
"This key means a program deliberately signalled failure with `(error ...)`. It
is NOT a bug in the interpreter and NOT a mystery — it is YOUR code (or a verb's
code) saying 'the precondition I needed did not hold'. The printed line joins the
message and the irritants with spaces: `boom 1 2`.

Because the message is author-chosen, the census cannot classify it into a
language family, so every custom `(error ...)` that escapes lands under the single
key `class:ErrorObject`. That makes the key a catch-all for deliberate,
program-signalled failure — a useful thing to watch: a rising `class:ErrorObject`
count means your own guards are firing more often."

  :common-causes (
    "a verb validating its arguments — `(if (< n 0) (error \"n must be >= 0\" n))`"
    "a precondition check in your own function that raises on bad input"
    "a `case`/`cond` with an `(else (error \"unreachable\" x))` fallthrough that
     turned out to be reachable"
    "a library surfacing a domain error through the standard `error` path")

  :at-the-verb
"The message names the failure but NOT always the verb — `(error ...)` messages
are free text. When you see `class:ErrorObject` rising, the fix is to read the
MESSAGE (via `error-object-message`) and the IRRITANTS (via
`error-object-irritants`), which usually name the offending value. If you author
verbs, prefix your `error` messages with `verb-namespace/name:` so the census
can route them to a per-verb key instead of this catch-all (see
misc-uncategorized for the routing rule)."

  :how-to-detect
"This is the ONE error family designed to be caught. `guard` on it gives you the
full object — message and irritants — as data:"
  :how-to-detect-code
"(display
  (guard (e ((error-object? e)
             (list 'msg (error-object-message e)
                   'irritants (error-object-irritants e))))
    (error \"boom\" 1 2)))"
  :how-to-detect-output "(msg boom irritants (1 2))"

  :fix-shape
"Two directions. If YOU raised it: the code is working as designed — decide
whether the caller should handle it (wrap in `guard`) or whether the precondition
should not have failed (fix the input upstream). If a VERB raised it: read the
message and irritants, correct the argument the message complains about. Never
'fix' it by removing the `error` call — that guard exists on purpose."

  :lesson
"`(error ...)` is the honest, standard, catchable way for code to refuse. Its
census key `class:ErrorObject` is where all such deliberate refusals pool. Two
takeaways: first, treat these as MESSAGES, not crashes — they carry structured
data (message + irritants) you should read, not guess around. Second, if you
write verbs, give your error messages a `namespace/name:` prefix so the census
can tell your verb's refusals apart instead of dumping them all here."

  :census-verbs (
    "(stats/error-count-of \"class:ErrorObject\")"
    "(stats/error-counts 10)")

  :related-cards ("class-RaisedValue" "misc-uncategorized"))
